/**
  @page guides-wngir Fitting a mesh interface with WNGIR
  @brief Mathematical model, solver stages, usage, diagnostics and extension points.

  @tableofcontents

  @section guides-wngir-setup Setup and ownership

  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::WNGIR::Problem fits marked mesh facets to a supplied zero level
  set. It writes accumulated displacement into the constructor's trial-function
  solution. It does not move vertices, replace mesh transformations, or remesh.
  The mesh is obtained from the displacement finite element space.

  The mesh, finite element space, trial and test functions must outlive the
  solver. Use matching vector-valued trial and test spaces on the local Eigen
  backend. Mark the interface facets and prepare the mesh connectivity before
  solving. The target gradient must be the derivative of the target level set
  at the displaced points; no target Hessian is required.

  @code{.cpp}
  // mesh already has its interface facets marked with Interface.
  P1<Math::SpatialVector<Real>, Geometry::LocalMesh> space(mesh, 2);
  // u owns the accumulated displacement; v tests increments on the same space.
  TrialFunction u(space);
  TestFunction v(space);
  Adaptation::WNGIR::Problem fitting(u, v);
  Adaptation::WNGIR::Parameters parameters;
  // The reference spacing stays fixed as the displacement changes.
  parameters.model.h = referenceSpacing;
  fitting.setParameters(parameters).setInterfaceAttribute(Interface);
  // Zero increments keep this boundary at its current position.
  fitting += DirichletBC(u, VectorFunction(Real(0), Real(0))).on(FixedBoundary);

  // Fit to a vertical line using its exact gradient; no Hessian is needed.
  RealFunction phi([](const Geometry::Point& point) {
    return point.x() - Real(0.55);
  });
  VectorFunction gradient(Real(1), Real(0));
  const auto report = fitting.solve(phi, gradient);
  // Check report.reason, geometricSup, minJ and maxQRel before using the result.
  // Applying displacement to mesh geometry is a separate application step.
  const auto& displacement = u.getSolution();
  @endcode

  Homogeneous boundary conditions hold the current boundary position fixed:
  they constrain each increment, not the accumulated displacement to zero.
  Nonzero increment values and identification conditions are rejected.
  For another solve on the same space, retain the solver and update its
  parameters or target. The existing displacement is the next initial guess.
  Create a new solver when replacing the mesh or finite element space.

  Applying the result is a separate application decision. Vertex displacement
  is appropriate for a linear geometric mesh. Higher-order geometry requires
  updating its transformations, not merely moving its corner vertices.

  For an affine simplicial mesh, @ref Rodin::Adaptation::WNGIR::Adapt owns the
  vector P1 space and fitting problem and applies valid displacements in place:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Prepare connectivity and mark interface facets before constructing adapt.
  Adaptation::WNGIR::Adapt adapt(mesh);
  Adaptation::WNGIR::Parameters controls;
  controls.model.h = referenceSpacing; // Keep the background scale fixed.
  adapt.setParameters(controls).setInterfaceAttribute(Interface);
  // Use the owned trial function for additional boundary conditions.
  auto& trial = adapt.getTrialFunction();
  adapt.getProblem() +=
    DirichletBC(trial, VectorFunction(Real(0), Real(0))).on(FixedBoundary);
  const auto adapted = adapt.execute(phi, gradient); // Mesh is updated here.
  @endcode

  Each execute call resets displacement to zero on the current mesh. It applies
  a result when `qualityBudgetSatisfied` is true, including valid best-effort
  exits, and leaves the mesh untouched otherwise. The report describes the
  fit relative to that call's starting mesh. The mesh must outlive the workflow;
  replacing connectivity requires a new workflow. Classification and remeshing
  remain application responsibilities. `getProblem()` provides the same metric,
  boundary-condition and monitor interfaces as an explicitly constructed problem.

  | Entry point | Context/backend | Status |
  |-------------|-----------------|--------|
  | `WNGIR::Problem(u, v)` | Supplied local Eigen trial/test storage | Supported, including higher-order spaces |
  | `WNGIR::Adapt(mesh)` | Local mesh, Eigen P1 storage | Supported for affine simplicial geometry |
  | `WNGIR::Problem(u, v)` | PETSc trial/test storage | Explicitly unsupported |
  | `WNGIR::Adapt(mesh)` | MPI mesh, requiring PETSc | Construction disabled pending distributed implementation |

  Backend selection follows storage and mesh context, not a runtime conversion.
  An MPI mesh will use PETSc when that specialization is implemented; it never
  falls back to Eigen. Renaming the API does not provide distributed assembly.

  @section guides-wngir-model Mathematical model

  The displacement metric is @f$M=F+D@f$: fitting observes normal motion on
  the marked interface, while distribution couples that motion to the volume.
  The metric defines the quadratic direction problem; it is not an additional
  energy minimized over the accumulated deformation. Quality is recovered
  through affine squared hinges on the increment and nonlinear checks on the
  accepted mesh, not through a separate shape-curvature term in the metric.

  For the current map @f$T_k(x)=x+u_k(x)@f$, set
  @f$A_k=I+\nabla u_k@f$, @f$j_k=\det A_k@f$ and
  @f$g_k=\nabla\phi(T_k(x))@f$. The two bilinear forms are
  @f[
    F_k[v,z]=\kappa_F G^{-2}\int_{\Gamma_0}
      (g_k\cdot v)(g_k\cdot z)\,dS_x,
    \qquad
  D_k[v,z]=h\int_{\Omega_k}\left[
    \kappa_{\rm dev}(\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(v)
         -\overline{\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(v)}):
        (\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(z)
         -\overline{\operatorname{dev}\varepsilon(z)})
    +\frac{\kappa_{\rm div}}{d}
        (\operatorname{div}v-\overline{\operatorname{div}v})
        (\operatorname{div}z-\overline{\operatorname{div}z})
    \right]dy.
  @f]
  Here @f$\Gamma_0@f$ and @f$\Omega_0@f$ are the reference interface and volume;
  @f$G@f$ is a fixed sampled target-gradient normalization. Fitting uses
  squared-residual curvature without the level-set Hessian or robust weights.
  The force and outer Armijo merit instead come from the fixed-scale Welsch
  objective
  @f[
    E(u)=G^{-2}\int_{\Gamma_0}\frac{\sigma^2}{2}
        \left(1-e^{-\phi(x+u(x))^2/\sigma^2}\right)\,dS_x,
    \qquad f_k=-DE(u_k).
  @f]
  The reference measure and robust scale @f$\sigma@f$ are held fixed during a
  solve. Thus the quadratic metric preconditions the robust descent; it does
  not replace the robust objective with a least-squares objective.

  Directional Newton scales the predictor before the affine hinge solve:
  @f$\bar p_k=a_kp_k@f$ and @f$\bar M_k=M_k/a_k@f$. The inner model is
  @f[
    \min_{v\in V_h}\left\{
       \tfrac12\bar M_k[v,v]-f_k[v]+B_k(v)\right\},
  @f]
  where @f$B_k@f$ is the squared-hinge penalty on frozen Jacobian and distortion
  slacks. Its stationarity residual controls inner convergence. The outer
  Armijo search then decreases @f$E@f$ while enforcing the actual sampled
  quality budget. See the @ref Rodin::Adaptation::WNGIR::Problem "WNGIR class reference"
  for the slack definitions, penalty scaling, Newton system, line search and
  geometric stopping equations.

  For current-coordinate fields, @f$\varepsilon(v)=\operatorname{sym}\nabla_yv@f$;
  the overbars are averages over the entire current domain @f$\Omega_k@f$.
  They are not per-element means. Both distribution coefficients are independent.

  @section guides-wngir-stages What the solver does

  Each outer iteration has four stages:

  1. Assemble the robust fitting force and the metric @f$M=F+D@f$.
     Fitting makes motion responsive to the target normal. Distribution couples
     neighboring elements through variation of current strain about its global
     mean. Coherent affine motions are free, including shear and anisotropic
     stretching. Spatial strain variation is resisted. This does not permit
     independent incompatible element motions; shared DOFs transmit motion.
  2. Solve for an unconstrained predictor. Directional Newton rescales the
     physical predictor and its quadratic metric before applying the hinges.
  3. Use native @ref Rodin::Solver::NewtonSolver to solve the frozen quadratic
     model with affine quadratic quality hinges. Inactive hinges need no Newton
     correction. Inner convergence uses the stationarity residual.
  4. Backtrack with Armijo on the robust fitting energy. Every trial must also
     satisfy the actual sampled Jacobian and distortion limits. Accept the
     displacement only after both checks pass.

  The retained linear adapter supports CG, SparseLU and optional MUMPS. MUMPS
  retains symbolic analysis when the sparsity pattern is unchanged. Numerical
  factors are reused only when the assembled operator is unchanged. Global
  centering is represented by a low-rank subtraction from the sparse core.
  Direct solvers factor the equivalent augmented system; CG applies the
  centered operator without a dense matrix. No gauge, mass penalty or
  stabilization is added. Singular systems remain subject to the selected
  backend. With both centered weights positive, distribution is coercive in
  H1 modulo global affine motions on a fixed connected bounded Lipschitz domain.
  The mesh-scale factor and changing domain preclude an unconditional uniform bound.

  @section guides-wngir-controls Main controls

  Model coefficients are grouped under `parameters.model`. Accuracy settings
  and work budgets are independent groups under `parameters.convergence`:
  `tolerance` and `iterations`. Globalization, linear-solver selection and
  quadrature have their own groups. All values in the following table are
  defaults except the required reference size.

  | Parameter | C++ | Description | Value |
  |-----------|-----|-------------|-------|
  | @f$h@f$ | `model.h` | Fixed background reference size | Required |
  | @f$\kappa_F@f$ | `model.fit` | Target-normal motion stiffness, not a force multiplier | @f$1@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_{\rm dev}@f$ | `model.distribution.deviatoric` | Cost of deviatoric-strain variation about its global mean | @f$10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_{\rm div}@f$ | `model.distribution.divergence` | Cost of divergence variation about its global mean, normalized by @f$d@f$ | @f$10^{-2}@f$ |
  | @f$\widehat\mu@f$ | `model.hinge` | Hinge strength relative to predicted fitting improvement | @f$10@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_J@f$ | `model.jacobianWeight` | Relative Jacobian-hinge row weight | @f$1@f$ |
  | @f$\kappa_Q@f$ | `model.distortionWeight` | Relative distortion-hinge row weight | @f$1@f$ |
  | @f$\gamma@f$ | `model.qualityGuard` | Guard fraction defining hinge activation margins | @f$0.1@f$ |
  | @f$j_{\mathrm{safe}}@f$ | `model.jacobian` | Relative Jacobian floor in the hinge and actual acceptance checks | @f$10^{-2}@f$ |
  | @f$Q_{\max}@f$ | `model.distortion` | Spendable relative-distortion budget | @f$10@f$ |
  | @f$\sigma@f$ | `model.robustScale` | Welsch robustness scale in level-set units | Automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$\lambda_{\max}@f$ | `globalization.maxStepOverH` | Optional predictor-motion limit divided by @f$h@f$; zero leaves directional Newton unrestricted | @f$0@f$ |
  | @f$c_A@f$ | `globalization.armijo` | Armijo sufficient-decrease coefficient | @f$10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_{\mathrm{geom}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.geometric` | Maximum sampled normalized-residual target | @f$h^{p+1}@f$, automatic (`0`) |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.innerRelative` | Inner stationarity tolerance relative to fitting force | @f$10^{-3}@f$ |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.innerAbsolute` | Absolute inner stationarity allowance | @f$10^{-12}@f$ |
  | @f$\tau_{\mathrm{lin}}@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.linearRelative` | Relative linear residual tolerance for every backend | @f$10^{-6}@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{outer}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.outer` | Maximum outer iterations | @f$30@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{inner}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.inner` | Maximum Newton corrections per outer iteration | @f$15@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{CG}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.linear` | Iteration cap per CG solve only | @f$1000@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{bt}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.backtracks` | Maximum outer trial halvings | @f$32@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_{\mathrm{step}}/h@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.stepOverH` | Small accepted-motion threshold for a best-effort exit | @f$5\times10^{-4}@f$ |
  | @f$\varepsilon_E@f$ | `convergence.tolerance.energy` | Relative energy-change threshold for a best-effort exit | @f$10^{-8}@f$ |
  | @f$N_{\mathrm{stag}}@f$ | `convergence.iterations.stagnation` | Consecutive small steps or energy changes before stagnation exit | @f$5@f$ |

  MUMPS is the default when compiled in; otherwise SparseLU is selected.
  Set `linear.solver` and `linear.threads` explicitly for reproducible runs.
  Quadrature orders are selected automatically unless overridden.
  Example flags use the same names as the C++ parameters: `--wngir-fit`,
  `--wngir-distribution-deviatoric`, `--wngir-distribution-divergence`,
  `--wngir-hinge`, `--wngir-jacobian` and `--wngir-distortion`.
  Iteration flags are `--wngir-outer-iterations` and `--wngir-inner-iterations`.
  Linear settings use `--wngir-linear-solver`, `--wngir-linear-threads` and
  `--wngir-linear-relative-tolerance`. Legacy spellings are rejected.

  @section guides-wngir-results Reading results

  Use `report.reason` rather than parsing a message. A geometric target hit is
  `WNGIR::Report::Reason::GeometricTarget`. Budget, small-step and small-energy
  exits are best-effort results, not geometric convergence. The stable text
  token is available through `report.getReasonString()`.

  The main geometric response is `geometricSup`, the maximum sampled normalized
  level-set residual on the complete interface, including its vertices.
  Normalization divides the absolute level-set value by the local target-gradient
  magnitude, giving a local distance estimate rather than a raw level-set value.
  The automatic target is @f$h^{p+1}@f$, using the reference spacing and the
  interface finite element order. This is not a certified Hausdorff error;
  a single target hit does not establish a refinement order.

  The report's `geometricConstant` is the dimensionless sampled score
  @f[
    C = \frac{D_\infty}{h_0^{p+1}}.
  @f]
  The background reference size @f$h_0@f$ remains fixed throughout fitting.
  This normalization is independent of a user-specified stopping tolerance.
  Geometry traces and canonical example responses expose the score as `geom_c`.

  Monitor `energy`, `geometricSup`, `geometricConstant`, `geometricSupTarget`, `minJ`, `maxQRel`,
  `iterations`, `innerIterations`, `lastInnerIterations`, `maxInnerIterations`
  and `innerRelativeResidual`. The report's `innerIterations` is the total
  correction count, whereas `parameters.convergence.iterations.inner` is the
  per-outer-iteration cap.
  `interfaceMeasure` is the reference integration measure used by the fitting
  energy. Energy and maximum error answer different questions; neither should
  silently replace the other as a stopping certificate.

  The quality budget is available to spend while fitting. A larger final
  distortion is not itself failure if it remains below the configured limit.
  `qualityBudgetSatisfied` records the sampled checks, not an everywhere-valid
  certificate for a curved element. Inspect the reason and quality fields even
  when the target was not reached.

  @code{.cpp}
  fitting.setMonitor([](const Adaptation::WNGIR::Report& report) {
    // Record or display report.energy, report.geometricSup and quality fields.
  });
  @endcode

  The monitor receives one snapshot per accepted step and one final report,
  including early returns. The final snapshot may repeat the last accepted
  step. It must not modify the problem or throw. `getReport()` holds the last
  completed solve. `trace` additionally enables the examples' diagnostic logs.

  @section guides-wngir-extension Adding metric terms

  `getMetric()` exposes a native additional bilinear form, not the internal
  scratch problem or a replacement for the canonical fitting and distribution.
  Both local and global bilinear integrators use Rodin's normal form interface.

  @code{.cpp}
  fitting.getMetric() += Integral(Dot(u, v));
  fitting.getMetric() -= Integral(Dot(u, v));
  @endcode

  Added terms are assembled again at each outer iteration. They change the
  quadratic direction model, not the robust energy or its fitting force.
  Maintain symmetry, nonnegative curvature and compatible unresolved modes.
  Arbitrary linear loads are deliberately not exposed: a load without a
  corresponding fitting energy would invalidate Armijo's descent model.

  @section guides-wngir-layout Implementation layout

  All method-specific code lives in `Adaptation/WNGIR/`:
  `Adapt.h` owns the P1 mesh workflow; `Problem.h` owns outer orchestration;
  `FittingCoefficient.h` and
  `FittingForce.h` assemble fitting; `Distribution.h` assembles distribution;
  `Hinge.h`, `HingeMetric.h`, `HingeForce.h` and `HingeProblem.h` define the
  inner model; `LinearSolver.h` adapts native linear solvers.
  `Parameters.h` and `Report.h` are the configuration and diagnostic surfaces.
  Geometry evaluation uses the shared adaptation deformation infrastructure.
*/
